    "body": "I also don't see any evidence that GMP is as Gemini has plucked out of thin air. I don't see any obvious #ifdefs reversing the order of iteration over the arrays, and that makes particular sense when it needs to be able to realloc them to not overflow, which would require moving the existing limbs were they not stored in little-endian order. This is also consistent with my memory from years ago when I had to delve into its internals to fix a library that was trying to be API-compatible.\r\n\r\nIn particular, mpz_export is helpful to look at. It lets you extract the binary representation of the number, as a series of specified-endianness words in a separately-specified order. The fast path (assuming the requested word size matches the limb size) is when `order == -1 && endian == HOST_ENDIAN`, which per the API documentation means \"least significant [word] first\" with each word in the \"native endianness of the host CPU\": https://github.com/gmp-mirror/gmp/blob/4b3b66d3328e390af894b97e0438aed90c3305be/mpz/export.c#L86-L92",
    "created_at": "2025-09-11T16:34:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-11T16:34:30Z",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/riscv-non-isa/riscv-elf-psabi-doc/pull/419#discussion_r2341707800",
